Yes. What you’re describing should be built as a LeaseOS Contact & Communications subsystem, but it should deliberately not behave like a normal phone contact app.

The key rule should be:

> LeaseOS stores operational relationships and approved work-contact information. It does not ingest or synchronize a worker’s personal address book.



That separation gives you the job-history intelligence you want without creating a huge privacy problem.

1. I’d divide the directory into five layers

Layer	What belongs there	Typical visibility

LeaseOS Platform Directory	LeaseOS support, billing, administration, safety/security escalation	Contracting-company admins; selected support options for users
Company Directory	Drivers, dispatchers, mechanics, safety, HR, supervisors, managers, owner, office	Controlled by company role
Partner / Operations Catalog	Consultants, client reps/company men, disposal facilities, medics, towing, recovery, vendors, shops, terminals, loading facilities	Dispatch/admin broadly; drivers when relevant
Job Contact Roster	Exact people/facilities relevant to one dispatched job	Assigned personnel and approved client users
Emergency Directory	911, CANUTEC, job ERAP number, company emergency contact, medic, client emergency contact, jurisdictional contacts	Always available where applicable


This is much safer than creating one giant contacts table and then trying to hide individual records afterward.

2. The Job Contact Roster becomes the important piece

When dispatch creates something like:

Job 26-00982 → Client A → Consultant B → Lease 12-34-...

LeaseOS automatically assembles:

Contact role	Example

Assigned driver	Dylan / work contact
Company dispatch	24-hour dispatch
Company safety	safety coordinator
Client representative	company man
Consultant	field consultant
On-site medic	medic unit / medic
Disposal	selected disposal facility
Loading location	facility/operator
ERAP	applicable ERAP holder
Emergency	911 / CANUTEC / other applicable authority
Mechanic	only if assigned/relevant
Supervisor	if included by dispatch


So instead of giving a driver the entire corporate address book, LeaseOS gives them the people they need for this job.

The client portal gets an even smaller projection of exactly the same data. A client could see the assigned driver, dispatcher, designated safety/emergency contact and perhaps company owner/operations manager if the contractor enables it. They would not see HR, unrelated mechanics, other drivers, payroll contacts, other clients, or your vendor directory.

3. Your past-job idea is worth building

I would call it Relationship History rather than simply “old contacts.”

Suppose a driver worked for the same consulting company six months ago. When that company appears again, LeaseOS could show:

Worked with before

ABC Consulting

John Smith — Field Consultant

Last worked together: Job 25-1837

Last verified: April 2026

4 previous jobs

Work phone

Work email

Previous locations worked together


But an old number should never silently be treated as current.

Give each contact:

active inactive superseded last_verified_at verified_by source organization_id

Then LeaseOS can say:

John Smith — number last verified 184 days ago

instead of pretending historical information is definitely current.

When dispatch creates another ABC Consulting job, LeaseOS can suggest:

“You have previously worked with these ABC Consulting contacts. Add one to this job?”

That is much better than automatically exposing everybody.

4. Drivers should have a personal “work history directory”

This is slightly different from the corporate directory.

A driver should be able to see approved work contacts associated with jobs they actually participated in.

For example:

My Work Contacts → ABC Oil → 7 previous jobs → John Smith

But they should not suddenly be able to search ABC Oil's entire employee list merely because their employer has that information elsewhere in LeaseOS.

That gives you the accumulated operational knowledge you're talking about while maintaining a tenant/privacy boundary.

5. Separate a person from their contact methods

I would not put phoneNumber directly on a person record.

Use something closer to:

Person
    ↓
Organization Membership
    ↓
Role
    ↓
Contact Methods

A single person might have:

John Smith
ABC Consulting

Role:
Field Consultant

Contact methods:
780-555-0101   work_mobile
780-555-0102   office
john@abc.ca    work_email
780-555-0199   emergency_only

And every contact method gets a privacy classification.

Classification	Meaning

public_work	Generally usable for business
internal_work	Company personnel only
job_only	Only participants on assigned jobs
client_visible	May appear to client
dispatch_only	Dispatch/admin
emergency_only	Revealed under defined emergency conditions
private_personal	Never exposed through LeaseOS operational views


That last category is important.

If an employee gives HR their personal home phone number, that does not mean it belongs in the driver's operational contact directory.

