But even here, permissions matter.

A dispatcher may need a driver's company cell number but doesn't necessarily need the driver's home address, emergency family contact, payroll information or HR notes.

9. Don't request the driver's phone contacts

This is one point where I would make LeaseOS intentionally restrictive.

Android classifies READ_CONTACTS as a dangerous permission. Google also recommends minimizing permissions. 

So LeaseOS should not do:

Grant access to your contacts
↓
upload phone address book
↓
merge everything into LeaseOS

Instead:

LeaseOS approved contact
        ↓
Tap phone number
        ↓
Android system dialer
        ↓
User presses Call

Android specifically supports this with ACTION_DIAL; unlike direct ACTION_CALL, it doesn't require LeaseOS to obtain the stronger CALL_PHONE permission. 

That is exactly how I would build the first version.

Later we could optionally have:

Add this LeaseOS work contact to my phone

but it should be a deliberate one-contact export initiated by the user, not LeaseOS reading their address book.

10. Emergency contacts should be special

I would put a red Emergency button in Field/Route that remains available offline.

Something like:

EMERGENCY
────────────────────
911
Call

Dangerous Goods
CANUTEC
Call

ERAP — This Load
ERAP Ref: 2-XXXX
Call ERAP Holder

Company Emergency
Call Safety

Site Emergency
Call Site Medic

Client Emergency
